sub1:assertion {
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/morphoeval-thompson1917-t1917-040> a pk:Claim ;
        rdfs:label "Thompson (1917) Dürer/Camper facial grids — whole-grid deformation test on human profile landmarks (Pass 1) faithfully reproduces Homo sapiens (soft-tissue mid-sagittal facial profile, 9 Wikimedia Commons photographs) morphology" ;
        dcterms:description "The simulation script(s) thompson/claims/t1917-040_on-the-theory-of-transformations.py implement the cited source model as published and produce forms that match real Homo sapiens (soft-tissue mid-sagittal facial profile, 9 Wikimedia Commons photographs) specimens and the source authors' figures. A belief under audit, not a fact: its credibility is carried by the MorphoEval rubric assessments." ;
        dcterms:subject <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-040_on-the-theory-of-transformations.py> ;
        pk:context "script(s): https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-040_on-the-theory-of-transformations.py; output: docs/media/t1917_040_on_the_theory_of_transformatio_comparison.png; organism: Homo sapiens (soft-tissue mid-sagittal facial profile, 9 Wikimedia Commons photographs); environment: n/a; claim_boundary: [reported_in_source: Dürer heads redrawn in rectangular (Fig. 364) and oblique (Fig. 366) nets | Camper's facial angle 'is the index to a general deformation' (p. 742 footnote); no measurements in the source; reconstructed_in_script: E1 stretch, E3 shear with k = -cot(omega), E4 affine, E5 tan(phi') = tan(phi) + k | similarity-vs-affine landmark fits on 4 accepted photographs (12 ordered pairs); transfer_boundary: 2-D soft-tissue silhouettes from photographs, not skulls or 3-D form | forehead often hidden by hair (g/n missing); does_not_claim: anything about populations or 'types' | growth or developmental mechanism]; other sources: On Growth and Form <https://archive.org/details/ongrowthform1917thom>, Vier Bücher von menschlicher Proportion <https://archive.org/details/hierinnsindbegri00dure>, Soft tissue cephalometric analysis for orthognathic surgery <https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/6932485/>; phenomenon_size: 979 µm (0.0009792 m, log10=-3.009 m, tier=5_primordium_100um); species_scale_ladder (20 runs from 979 µm to 2.85 m): 979 µm:cortical-sulcal-axon-tension, 1.4 mm:bressloff-cowan-V1-hallucination, 1.5 mm:corticothalamic-seizure-spirals, 1.7 mm:homo-sapiens-bioelectric-polarity, 3.1 mm:vertex-glass-jamming, 3.4 mm:homo-sapiens-vertex-packing, 6.3 cm:homo-sapiens-turing-wavelength, 11.7 cm:homo-sapiens-clock-wavefront" ;
        pk:derivedFromPaper <https://doi.org/10.1186/1746-160X-10-54>,
            <https://doi.org/10.5061/dryad.0zpc86746> .

    <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib/blob/main/thompson/claims/t1917-040_on-the-theory-of-transformations.py> a schema:SoftwareSourceCode ;
        rdfs:label "thompson/claims/t1917-040_on-the-theory-of-transformations.py" ;
        schema:codeRepository <https://github.com/fractastical/morpholib> ;
        schema:programmingLanguage "Python" .
}

